<script>

const topics = {

1: {
title: "Number Bases",

lesson: `
<p>A number base is a system used to represent numbers using a particular set of digits.</p>

<p>The decimal system is base 10 and uses the digits 0 to 9. Other common bases include binary (base 2), ternary (base 3), quaternary (base 4), octal (base 8) and hexadecimal (base 16).</p>

<p>In base 2, only 0 and 1 are used. In base 5, the digits 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 are used.</p>

<div class="formula">
(234)<sub>5</sub> = 2(5<sup>2</sup>) + 3(5<sup>1</sup>) + 4(5<sup>0</sup>)
</div>

<p>To convert from another base to base 10, multiply each digit by the appropriate power of the base and add.</p>

<p>To convert a decimal number to another base, repeatedly divide by the new base and read the remainders from bottom to top.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Convert (1011)<sub>2</sub> to base 10.</p>
<p>= 1(2<sup>3</sup>) + 0(2<sup>2</sup>) + 1(2<sup>1</sup>) + 1(2<sup>0</sup>)</p>
<p>= 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = <b>11</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Convert 25<sub>10</sub> to base 2.</p>
<p>25 ÷ 2 = 12 remainder 1</p>
<p>12 ÷ 2 = 6 remainder 0</p>
<p>6 ÷ 2 = 3 remainder 0</p>
<p>3 ÷ 2 = 1 remainder 1</p>
<p>1 ÷ 2 = 0 remainder 1</p>
<p>Therefore 25<sub>10</sub> = <b>11001<sub>2</sub></b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Convert (11010)<sub>2</sub> to base 10.</li>
<li>Convert (243)<sub>5</sub> to base 10.</li>
<li>Convert 18<sub>10</sub> to base 2.</li>
<li>Convert 45<sub>10</sub> to base 5.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>26<sub>10</sub></li>
<li>73<sub>10</sub></li>
<li>10010<sub>2</sub></li>
<li>140<sub>5</sub></li>
</ol>
`
},

2: {
title: "Fractions and Decimals",

lesson: `
<p>A fraction represents part of a whole. It has a numerator and denominator.</p>

<p>Fractions may be proper, improper or mixed numbers. To add or subtract fractions, first find a common denominator.</p>

<p>To multiply fractions, multiply the numerators and multiply the denominators. To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal.</p>

<p>A decimal can be terminating or recurring. Fractions can be converted to decimals by division.</p>

<div class="formula">
Fraction = Numerator ÷ Denominator
</div>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Evaluate 2/3 + 1/6.</p>
<p>LCM of 3 and 6 is 6.</p>
<p>2/3 = 4/6.</p>
<p>Therefore 4/6 + 1/6 = <b>5/6</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Evaluate 3/4 × 2/5.</p>
<p>= 6/20 = <b>3/10</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Convert 0.75 to a fraction.</p>
<p>0.75 = 75/100 = <b>3/4</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Calculate 3/5 + 1/10.</li>
<li>Calculate 7/8 - 1/4.</li>
<li>Calculate 2/3 × 9/10.</li>
<li>Convert 0.625 to a fraction.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>7/10</li>
<li>5/8</li>
<li>3/5</li>
<li>5/8</li>
</ol>
`
},

3: {
title: "Approximation and Estimation",

lesson: `
<p>Approximation means giving a number close to its exact value. Common methods include rounding to a given number of decimal places or significant figures.</p>

<p>For decimal places, count digits after the decimal point. For significant figures, begin counting from the first non-zero digit.</p>

<p>If the next digit is 5 or greater, increase the last retained digit by 1. If it is less than 5, leave the retained digit unchanged.</p>

<p>Estimation involves replacing numbers by convenient approximate values to obtain a quick answer.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Round 47.386 to 2 decimal places.</p>
<p>The third decimal digit is 6, so 47.38 becomes <b>47.39</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Round 0.004827 to 2 significant figures.</p>
<p>The first two significant figures are 4 and 8. The next digit is 2.</p>
<p>Answer = <b>0.0048</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Estimate 49.8 × 20.2.</p>
<p>Use 50 × 20 = <b>1000</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Round 38.746 to 2 decimal places.</li>
<li>Round 0.006438 to 2 significant figures.</li>
<li>Estimate 198 × 51.</li>
<li>Estimate 402 ÷ 19.8.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>38.75</li>
<li>0.0064</li>
<li>About 10,000</li>
<li>About 20</li>
</ol>
`
},

4: {
title: "Standard Form",

lesson: `
<p>Standard form is used to write very large or very small numbers conveniently.</p>

<div class="formula">
A × 10<sup>n</sup>, where 1 ≤ A &lt; 10
</div>

<p>A positive exponent is used for numbers greater than or equal to 10, while a negative exponent is used for numbers between 0 and 1.</p>

<p>When multiplying numbers in standard form, multiply the coefficients and add the powers of 10.</p>

<p>When dividing, divide the coefficients and subtract the powers.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Write 4500000 in standard form.</p>
<p>= <b>4.5 × 10<sup>6</sup></b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Write 0.00032 in standard form.</p>
<p>= <b>3.2 × 10<sup>-4</sup></b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> (3 × 10<sup>4</sup>)(2 × 10<sup>3</sup>)</p>
<p>= 6 × 10<sup>7</sup>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Write 720000 in standard form.</li>
<li>Write 0.000056 in standard form.</li>
<li>Evaluate (4 × 10<sup>5</sup>)(3 × 10<sup>2</sup>).</li>
<li>Evaluate (8 × 10<sup>6</sup>) ÷ (2 × 10<sup>3</sup>).</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>7.2 × 10<sup>5</sup></li>
<li>5.6 × 10<sup>-5</sup></li>
<li>1.2 × 10<sup>8</sup></li>
<li>4 × 10<sup>3</sup></li>
</ol>
`
},

5: {
title: "Logarithms",

lesson: `
<p>A logarithm is another way of expressing an index.</p>

<div class="formula">
If a<sup>x</sup> = N, then log<sub>a</sub>N = x.
</div>

<p>The logarithm laws are:</p>
<ul>
<li>log(ab) = log a + log b</li>
<li>log(a/b) = log a - log b</li>
<li>log(a<sup>n</sup>) = n log a</li>
</ul>

<p>Common logarithms have base 10. The logarithm of 1 to any valid base is 0.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Evaluate log<sub>10</sub>1000.</p>
<p>Since 10<sup>3</sup> = 1000, the answer is <b>3</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Solve log<sub>2</sub>x = 5.</p>
<p>x = 2<sup>5</sup> = <b>32</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Simplify log 100 + log 10.</p>
<p>= log(100 × 10) = log 1000 = <b>3</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Evaluate log<sub>10</sub>100.</li>
<li>Evaluate log<sub>2</sub>16.</li>
<li>Solve log<sub>3</sub>x = 4.</li>
<li>Simplify log 1000 - log 10.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>2</li>
<li>4</li>
<li>x = 81</li>
<li>2</li>
</ol>
`
},

6: {
title: "Indices",

lesson: `
<p>Indices, also called powers or exponents, show how many times a number is multiplied by itself.</p>

<div class="formula">
a<sup>m</sup> × a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m+n</sup>
</div>

<div class="formula">
a<sup>m</sup> ÷ a<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>m-n</sup>
</div>

<div class="formula">
(a<sup>m</sup>)<sup>n</sup> = a<sup>mn</sup>
</div>

<p>Also, a<sup>0</sup> = 1 for a ≠ 0, and a<sup>-n</sup> = 1/a<sup>n</sup>.</p>

<p>Fractional indices are related to roots. For example, a<sup>1/2</sup> = √a.</p>

<p>An indicial equation is an equation in which the unknown occurs as an exponent.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Simplify 2<sup>3</sup> × 2<sup>4</sup>.</p>
<p>= 2<sup>7</sup> = <b>128</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Simplify 5<sup>7</sup> ÷ 5<sup>3</sup>.</p>
<p>= 5<sup>4</sup> = <b>625</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Solve 2<sup>x</sup> = 32.</p>
<p>Since 32 = 2<sup>5</sup>, x = <b>5</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 4:</b> Solve 3<sup>2x</sup> = 81.</p>
<p>81 = 3<sup>4</sup>, therefore 2x = 4 and x = <b>2</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Simplify 3<sup>4</sup> × 3<sup>2</sup>.</li>
<li>Simplify 7<sup>6</sup> ÷ 7<sup>2</sup>.</li>
<li>Solve 5<sup>x</sup> = 125.</li>
<li>Solve 2<sup>2x</sup> = 64.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>3<sup>6</sup> = 729</li>
<li>7<sup>4</sup> = 2401</li>
<li>x = 3</li>
<li>x = 3</li>
</ol>
`
},

7: {
title: "Surds",

lesson: `
<p>A surd is an irrational root that cannot be simplified to a rational number.</p>

<p>Examples include √2, √3 and √5.</p>

<p>To simplify a surd, express the number under the root as a product containing a perfect square.</p>

<div class="formula">
√(ab) = √a × √b
</div>

<p>Like surds can be added or subtracted by combining their coefficients. Surds can also be rationalised when they occur in denominators.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Simplify √50.</p>
<p>√50 = √(25 × 2) = <b>5√2</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Simplify 3√2 + 5√2.</p>
<p>= <b>8√2</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Rationalise 1/√3.</p>
<p>Multiply top and bottom by √3:</p>
<p>= √3/3.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Simplify √72.</li>
<li>Simplify 4√5 + 3√5.</li>
<li>Simplify √12 + √27.</li>
<li>Rationalise 2/√5.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>6√2</li>
<li>7√5</li>
<li>5√3</li>
<li>2√5/5</li>
</ol>
`
},

8: {
title: "Sets",

lesson: `
<p>A set is a well-defined collection of objects. Objects in a set are called elements.</p>

<p>Sets may be represented by listing their elements or by using set-builder notation.</p>

<p>Important terms include universal set, empty set, subset, union and intersection.</p>

<div class="formula">
A ∪ B = elements in A or B
</div>

<div class="formula">
A ∩ B = elements common to A and B
</div>

<p>Venn diagrams are useful for representing relationships between sets.</p>
`,

example: `
<p>Let A = {1,2,3,4} and B = {3,4,5,6}.</p>
<p>A ∪ B = <b>{1,2,3,4,5,6}</b>.</p>
<p>A ∩ B = <b>{3,4}</b>.</p>
<p>A is a subset of a universal set containing all these elements.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>If A={1,2,3} and B={3,4,5}, find A∪B.</li>
<li>Find A∩B.</li>
<li>How many elements are in {a,b,c,d,e}?</li>
<li>State whether {1,2} is a subset of {1,2,3}.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>{1,2,3,4,5}</li>
<li>{3}</li>
<li>5</li>
<li>Yes</li>
</ol>
`
},

9: {
title: "Algebraic Expressions",

lesson: `
<p>An algebraic expression consists of numbers, variables and operations. Terms are separated by plus or minus signs.</p>

<p>Like terms have the same variables raised to the same powers.</p>

<p>To simplify an expression, collect like terms and perform the indicated operations.</p>

<p>The distributive law states that a(b+c) = ab+ac.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Simplify 3x + 5x - 2.</p>
<p>= <b>8x - 2</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Expand 4(x+3).</p>
<p>= 4x + 12.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Simplify 3a + 2b - a + 5b.</p>
<p>= <b>2a + 7b</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Simplify 7x + 2x - 4.</li>
<li>Expand 5(y+2).</li>
<li>Simplify 4a+3b-2a+b.</li>
<li>Expand 3(2x-5).</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>9x - 4</li>
<li>5y + 10</li>
<li>2a + 4b</li>
<li>6x - 15</li>
</ol>
`
},

10: {
title: "Factorization",

lesson: `
<p>Factorization is the process of expressing an algebraic expression as a product of factors.</p>

<p>Common methods include taking out the common factor, grouping, difference of two squares and factorising quadratic expressions.</p>

<div class="formula">
a² - b² = (a-b)(a+b)
</div>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Factorise 6x + 12.</p>
<p>= <b>6(x+2)</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Factorise x² - 9.</p>
<p>= (x-3)(x+3).</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Factorise x² + 5x + 6.</p>
<p>Two numbers with product 6 and sum 5 are 2 and 3.</p>
<p>Therefore = <b>(x+2)(x+3)</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Factorise 8x+16.</li>
<li>Factorise x²-25.</li>
<li>Factorise x²+7x+12.</li>
<li>Factorise 2x²+6x.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>8(x+2)</li>
<li>(x-5)(x+5)</li>
<li>(x+3)(x+4)</li>
<li>2x(x+3)</li>
</ol>
`
},

11: {
title: "Linear Equations",

lesson: `
<p>A linear equation is an equation in which the highest power of the unknown is 1.</p>

<p>The aim of solving an equation is to find the value of the unknown that makes the equation true.</p>

<p>Whatever operation is performed on one side of an equation must also be performed on the other side.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Solve 3x + 5 = 20.</p>
<p>3x = 15.</p>
<p>x = <b>5</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Solve 5x - 7 = 18.</p>
<p>5x = 25.</p>
<p>x = <b>5</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Solve 2x+7=19.</li>
<li>Solve 4x-9=15.</li>
<li>Solve 7x+3=31.</li>
<li>Solve 3(x+2)=21.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>x=6</li>
<li>x=6</li>
<li>x=4</li>
<li>x=5</li>
</ol>
`
},

12: {
title: "Change of Subject of Formula",

lesson: `
<p>Changing the subject of a formula means rearranging a formula so that a different variable is alone on one side.</p>

<p>The same algebraic operations used in solving equations are used here.</p>

<p>Care must be taken when a variable occurs in more than one term or inside brackets.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> Make x the subject of y = x + 5.</p>
<p>x = <b>y - 5</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> Make r the subject of A = πr².</p>
<p>r² = A/π.</p>
<p>r = <b>√(A/π)</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 3:</b> Make v the subject of s = ut + 1/2at².</p>
<p>ut = s - 1/2at².</p>
<p>u = (s - 1/2at²)/t.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Make x the subject of y=3x+2.</li>
<li>Make a the subject of v=u+at.</li>
<li>Make r the subject of C=2πr.</li>
<li>Make h the subject of A=1/2bh.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>x=(y-2)/3</li>
<li>a=(v-u)/t</li>
<li>r=C/(2π)</li>
<li>h=2A/b</li>
</ol>
`
},

13: {
title: "Simultaneous Equations",

lesson: `
<p>Simultaneous equations are two or more equations involving two or more unknowns that must be satisfied at the same time.</p>

<p>Common methods are substitution and elimination.</p>

<p>In the elimination method, coefficients are made equal so that one variable can be eliminated.</p>
`,

example: `
<p>Solve:</p>
<p>x + y = 10</p>
<p>x - y = 2</p>

<p>Add the equations:</p>
<p>2x = 12, so x = 6.</p>
<p>Substitute into x+y=10:</p>
<p>6+y=10, therefore y=<b>4</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>Solve x+y=9 and x-y=3.</li>
<li>Solve 2x+y=11 and x+y=7.</li>
<li>Solve x+2y=8 and x-y=2.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>x=6, y=3</li>
<li>x=4, y=3</li>
<li>x=4, y=2</li>
</ol>
`
},

14: {
title: "Quadratic Equations",

lesson: `
<p>A quadratic equation is an equation of the form ax²+bx+c=0, where a is not zero.</p>

<p>Quadratic equations can be solved by factorization, completing the square or the quadratic formula.</p>

<div class="formula">
x = [-b ± √(b²-4ac)] / 2a
</div>

<p>The expression b²-4ac is called the discriminant.</p>
`,

example: `
