15: {
title: "Variation",

lesson: `
<p>Variation describes how one quantity changes in relation to another.</p>

<p>In direct variation, y is proportional to x:</p>

<div class="formula">y = kx</div>

<p>In inverse variation:</p>

<div class="formula">y = k/x</div>

<p>Joint variation involves more than one variable, while combined variation may involve direct and inverse relationships together.</p>
`,

example: `
<p><b>Example 1:</b> y varies directly as x. If y=12 when x=4, find y when x=10.</p>
<p>12=4k, so k=3.</p>
<p>Therefore y=3x.</p>
<p>When x=10, y=<b>30</b>.</p>

<p><b>Example 2:</b> y varies inversely as x. If y=6 when x=4, find y when x=8.</p>
<p>yx=k=24.</p>
<p>y=24/8=<b>3</b>.</p>
`,

practice: `
<ol>
<li>y varies directly as x. If y=15 when x=5, find y when x=8.</li>
<li>y varies inversely as x. If y=10 when x=3, find y when x=6.</li>
<li>If y=kx and y=20 when x=4, find k.</li>
</ol>
`,

answer: `
<ol>
<li>24</li>
<li>5</li>
<li>5</li>
</ol>
`
},

16: {
